# gdb — target-native debugger; runs ON the i486 disc (ptrace-based local
# process debugging + gdbserver), not a cross-host remote debugger. Pulls
# dev-libs/gmp + dev-libs/mpfr (unconditional) and dev-libs/expat (USE=xml).
# See .batteries/reports/gdb.md for the musl thread-debugging caveats.
dev-debug/gdb

# Scripting languages
dev-lang/lua

# perl — static, -Uusedl (no dynamic loader on the disc, so no XS .so loading;
# any XS module would need to be baked into the binary via static_ext — see
# monolith-perl ebuild). Built with arsv/perl-cross driving vanilla perl
# (Gentoo's dev-lang/perl Configure is cross-hostile). No threads, no CPAN
# client. CGI.pm 4.10 (removed from perl core in 5.22) is baked in as a
# PURE-PERL rider for the guestbook's param()/header() — pinned specifically
# to 4.10 because every CGI.pm release from 4.11 onward hard-depends on
# HTML::Entities, which ships inside the XS HTML-Parser distribution; 4.10 is
# the newest release with zero XS in its dependency tree (verified against
# its Makefile.PL and a tarball-wide *.xs/*.c/*.h grep).
# DBI + DBD::SQLite are DEFERRED, not shipped: this is a hard perl-cross
# limitation, not a fallback-not-yet-taken. DBD::SQLite's Makefile.PL needs
# an already-importable DBI at ITS OWN configure time (it calls into
# DBI::DBD's helper routines), but perl-cross's static_ext bake is a single
# pass over the whole perl build — DBI is still mid-build, nothing to import
# yet. There is no dynamic loader on this disc to build DBI first and
# `use DBI` it into a second pass either. The guestbook therefore talks to
# SQLite via the on-disc `sqlite3` CLI (dev-db/monolith-sqlite, P1) instead
# of DBI — shelling out, not linking in.
dev-lang/monolith-perl

